We compute \(f(x)=x^{2}+x+41\) for positive integers \(x\) starting from \(1\) until it is no longer prime.

\[
\begin{aligned}
x=1&:\quad f(1)=1^2+1+41=43 \quad \text{(prime)}\\
x=2&:\quad f(2)=2^2+2+41=47 \quad \text{(prime)}\\
x=3&:\quad f(3)=3^2+3+41=53 \quad \text{(prime)}\\
x=4&:\quad f(4)=4^2+4+41=57 \quad \text{(not prime)}
\end{aligned}
\]

Since \(57\) is composite (in fact \(57=3\cdot 19\)), the smallest positive integer \(x\) for which \(x^{2}+x+41\) is not prime is \(\boxed{4}\).

